{"title":"2016 OIM Problems/Problem 4","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_OIM_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find the greatest number of bishops that can be placed on an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/b/43bb73e4f0a880ad6bdaf6948c771579951be92b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 \\times 8$\" width=\"40\" height=\"12\" /> chess board, so that no two bishops are on the same square and each bishop is threatened at most by one of the other bishops.\n</p><p><b>Note.</b> A bishop threatens another if both are on two different squares of the same diagonal. The board has as diagonals the two main diagonals and those parallel to them.\n</p><p>~translated into English by Tomas Diaz. ~orders@tomasdiaz.com\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_OIM_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A bishop attacks along diagonals, so two bishops threaten each other if and only if they lie on the same diagonal. Color the board in the standard checkerboard pattern. Since bishops on opposite colors never interact, we may consider the two colors independently and sum the results.\n</p><p>We analyze the black squares. These lie on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> diagonals in each direction. Label one family of diagonals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/c/d7cc564546fbefb7e041eeb9cf4b61fb47993d60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_1, D_2, \\dots, D_7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"118\" height=\"18\" /> and the other <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/a/56aef7525d0bd61aafb74c1eedd088290ccad498.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_1, E_2, \\dots, E_7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"18\" />. Each square lies at the intersection of exactly one <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/f/8dfcbccbc64a91b700ee90d800d2d0a0c2cda992.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"17\" /> and one <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/7/687307b5034ee502c54f273e4295f8bfc3ec3ee7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"22\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/0/9a0ac59ec3562b84367674cfe6245f223b892913.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"15\" /> be the number of bishops placed on diagonal <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/f/8dfcbccbc64a91b700ee90d800d2d0a0c2cda992.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"17\" />. Since any two bishops on the same diagonal threaten each other, each bishop on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/f/8dfcbccbc64a91b700ee90d800d2d0a0c2cda992.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"17\" /> is threatened by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/0/d50e6cd95e2706b3a37552d088b60340f3e66c5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_i - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"48\" height=\"17\" /> bishops on that diagonal. Because each bishop can be threatened by at most one other bishop, we must have\n\\[\nk_i - 1 \\le 1 \\Rightarrow k_i \\le 2\n\\]\n</p><p>Now suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/f/7ef1228583b73154dd3bfd76a19422f773a1ab99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_i = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" /> for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />. Let the two bishops lie at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/4/884a0de6413c4bf2ffeee1c981d71050bab05f2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(D_i, E_a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/4/88499a3ac511c6dc1d5d3f7d76f5eec4c483759a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(D_i, E_b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"65\" height=\"20\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/3/dc3c11a9c0b3b892676261e6c765abdd58cc2b51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\ne b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"17\" />. These two bishops already threaten each other, so each has exactly one threat. Therefore, neither bishop can lie on any other diagonal containing a bishop. In particular, the diagonals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/a/56a82f7785e24c275a5c85ce43d56fa4c2b49b1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/d/94d90a97dbc3a70b3f5541a8f3f12629c24c3c69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E_b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> contain no other bishops.\n</p><p>Thus, each diagonal <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/f/8dfcbccbc64a91b700ee90d800d2d0a0c2cda992.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"17\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/f/7ef1228583b73154dd3bfd76a19422f773a1ab99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_i = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" /> occupies two distinct diagonals in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />-direction that cannot contain any additional bishops.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> be the number of diagonals with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/f/7ef1228583b73154dd3bfd76a19422f773a1ab99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_i = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be the number with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/1/ed1de2bf86e528370b757ea28c2be0ff18aebaf9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_i = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" />. Then the total number of bishops is\n\\[\n2x + y\n\\]\n</p><p>The <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> diagonals with two bishops occupy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/c/2ec701493fa72dda5d6747fe14f30e7b1c2fb6c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> diagonals in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />-direction, leaving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/d/64dbd03c06e122fa4ce3ca323f142d723533109d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 - 2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> diagonals available. Each of these can contain at most one bishop, so\n\\[\ny \\le 7 - 2x\n\\]\n</p><p>Therefore,\n\\[\n2x + y \\le 2x + (7 - 2x) = 7\n\\]\n</p><p>Hence, at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> bishops can be placed on black squares. By symmetry, the same holds for white squares, giving a total of\n\\[\n7 + 7 = \\boxed{14}\n\\]\n</p><p>This bound is achievable by placing one bishop on each diagonal of both colors, so the maximum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/f/06faeb7808a02885011d393c56d69fa3f35b285e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{14}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"23\" />.\n<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=~Trashboycan\" title=\"~Trashboycan\">~Trashboycan</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_OIM_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=OIM_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"OIM Problems and Solutions\">OIM Problems and Solutions</a>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018002200\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.016 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.047 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 159/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5207/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:26266:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018002200 and revision id 280724. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}